Anyone know about this?

I posted recently about a restoration bug in which mread was saying that
it expected to read n bytes, but got x bytes, where n was some huge number.

It was pointed out to me that this was a symptom of some arrays being 
initialized to null such like:

char foo[100] = 0;

whereas it would only initialize the first element and leave the rest as
garbage.

The closest I could find was

#define DUMMY {0}

... and later ...

char foo[100] = DUMMY; /* for example */

and I would think that this would work.

In any case, I put in some strategic pline()s, and found that the bug is
that in o_name.c, where it's restoring the names, at one point it reads
into len some huge number... so I assume that the problem is in the code
where is SAVES the name list, it's checking objects[i].oc_uname and finding
that it's not NULL, and since it's garbage, writing out some garbagely huge
number.

Unfortunately, I couldn't find where oc_uname is initialized.

Anyone? Anyone? Buehler?


